witness|observer|NOUN|person who saw an event|A person who saw an event happen and can describe it.|nhân chứng; người chứng kiến|A witness described the car that left the scene.|Một nhân chứng mô tả chiếc xe rời khỏi hiện trường.|Countable; witness to an event names what was observed; witnessing does not guarantee a complete account.|0
witness|see-happen|VERB|see an event take place|To see an event happen, especially something significant.|chứng kiến|We witnessed the opening of the new community centre.|Chúng tôi chứng kiến lễ khai trương trung tâm cộng đồng mới.|Transitive; witness an event has a direct object, unlike be a witness to an event.|1
approximate|not-exact|ADJECTIVE|close to an exact amount|Near the correct amount or value but not measured or stated exactly.|gần đúng; xấp xỉ|Please give an approximate cost before we order the materials.|Hãy cho biết chi phí ước tính trước khi chúng tôi đặt vật liệu.|Before a noun or after a linking verb; an approximate figure is an estimate, not an exact measurement.|-
honour|privilege|NOUN|valued privilege|A privilege or opportunity that makes someone feel proud or respected.|vinh dự|It was an honour to introduce the visiting poet.|Được giới thiệu nhà thơ đến thăm là một vinh dự.|Countable in an honour to do something; the initial h is silent, so the article is an.|0
honour|respect|NOUN|public respect and recognition|Respect and recognition given to someone because of their achievements or character.|sự kính trọng; sự tôn vinh|The town held a concert in honour of its volunteer nurses.|Thị trấn tổ chức một buổi hòa nhạc để tôn vinh các y tá tình nguyện.|Uncountable in this sense; in honour of introduces the person being recognized.|1
honour|recognize|VERB|show respect publicly|To show respect or recognition for someone, often through a ceremony or award.|tôn vinh|The school honoured the coach at the end of the season.|Nhà trường tôn vinh huấn luyện viên vào cuối mùa giải.|Transitive honour someone; an award or public event can show the recognition.|2
honour|integrity|NOUN|sense of moral integrity|The quality of keeping moral principles and acting in a way worthy of respect.|danh dự; phẩm chất chính trực|He refused to break the promise because his honour mattered to him.|Anh ấy từ chối thất hứa vì coi trọng danh dự của mình.|Uncountable; a matter of honour concerns moral integrity, not merely a prize.|3
honour|keep-commitment|VERB|fulfil an obligation|To do what a promise, agreement, or obligation requires.|thực hiện đúng cam kết|The supplier honoured the agreement and replaced the damaged chairs.|Nhà cung cấp thực hiện đúng thỏa thuận và thay những chiếc ghế bị hỏng.|Transitive; objects include a promise, agreement, or commitment rather than the person being celebrated.|4
merry|cheerful|ADJECTIVE|happy and lively|Feeling or showing cheerful enjoyment.|vui vẻ; hớn hở|Merry voices drifted out of the kitchen as the family cooked together.|Tiếng nói cười vui vẻ vọng ra từ bếp khi cả gia đình cùng nấu ăn.|Can describe people, voices, or an atmosphere; not limited to a holiday greeting.|-
omit|leave-out|VERB|leave something out|To leave something out of an account, list, or action, intentionally or by mistake.|bỏ sót; lược bỏ|The invitation omitted the address of the meeting room.|Giấy mời bỏ sót địa chỉ phòng họp.|Transitive omit something; omit to do something is a formal construction for failing to do it.|-
